# {{FACTORY_NAME}}

A [jigs](https://github.com/salimhamed/jigs) factory: workflows, repository
bindings, and the service that runs them.

## Layout

- `jigs.config.ts` — service settings, bindings, deferred workflow registrations,
  and schedules. Secrets live in `.env`.
- `jigs.ts` — generated factory-local durable steps and bound jigs blocks.
  Commit this file. Refresh it with `jigs generate`; custom code belongs elsewhere.
- `workflows/` — complete processes with a `"use workflow"` entry point.
- `blocks/` — reusable coordination and replay-safe decisions, without directives.
- `steps/` — add custom `"use step"` functions here for durable external operations.
- `blocks/tickets/` — explicit ticket acquisition and mapping to delivery requirements.
- `jigs.config.test.ts` — factory declarations and emitted workflow/step ids.
- `workflows/ship.test.ts` — what the workflow body hands delivery for a given input.
- `docker-compose.yml` — the factory's Postgres World.

## Setup

```sh
cp .env.example .env      # fill in LINEAR_API_KEY and GITHUB_TOKEN
pnpm install
pnpm exec jigs up
pnpm exec jigs bind git@github.com:owner/repo.git
pnpm exec jigs up --restart
```

Every `jigs` below is this factory's own: `pnpm exec jigs …`.
The service listens on `:{{SERVICE_PORT}}`, its dashboard on
`:{{DASHBOARD_PORT}}`, its World on `:{{POSTGRES_PORT}}`.

`jigs bind` and `jigs unbind` edit the literal `bindings` object in
`jigs.config.ts`. If the configuration computes bindings or otherwise cannot
be edited unambiguously, they explain the required manual change and leave
configuration and webhooks untouched.

## Running a workflow

```sh
jigs run ship --input ticket=ABC-123 --input binding=repo --input merge=human
jigs ps
jigs logs <run>
jigs cancel <run>
```

`ship` reviews a ticket, implements and reviews the change, opens a pull request,
responds to reviews and CI, then tears down the worktree after merge.
`merge=jigs` squash-merges on approval; `merge=human` waits for a person to merge.

`<run>` is a run id, a unique prefix, or the ticket the run claimed.
Runs outlive the launching terminal. `jigs doctor` checks prerequisites;
every launch also preflights before creating a run.

## Development

```sh
pnpm typecheck
jigs build
pnpm test
```

`pnpm test` checks the last build's emitted ids, so build first. Builds refuse
missing or modified generated `jigs.ts` files: run `jigs generate`, review,
and commit the result.

To add a workflow, export its function and input schema from `workflows/`,
and default-export `{ workflow: yourWorkflow, inputs: yourInputs }` (with
optional `requires`). Register a deferred loader in `jigs.config.ts`:

```ts
workflows: {
  ship: () => import("./workflows/ship.ts"),
}
```

Factory code imports from the project root, not by relative depth. The
`imports` map in `package.json` anchors three names:

```ts
import { deliverChange } from "#jigs";
import { acquireLinearTicket } from "#blocks/tickets/linear";
import { publishPullRequest } from "#steps/publish";
```

`#jigs` is the generated file, `#blocks/<path>` and `#steps/<path>` mirror those
directories, and the target's `.ts` extension lives in the map rather than at the
import. Add a mapping for any directory you add. The deferred workflow loaders in
`jigs.config.ts` stay relative: they register workflow files for the build rather
than being import sites.

Import pure builders such as `claude`, `codex`, and `claimTicket` from
`@salimhamed/jigs/blocks`. Custom steps import implementations from
`@salimhamed/jigs/steps` inside a factory module containing named `"use step"`
functions.

`ship` exposes `implementationHarness`, `implementationModel`, `reviewHarness`,
`reviewModel`, `implementationReviewRounds`, `ciFixAttempts`, and
`pullRequestRevisionRounds` inputs. A model left unset takes the chosen
harness's own default from `defaultModels` in `workflows/ship.ts`.
Outcomes are `merged`, `closed`, `limit-reached`, `stopped`, and
`uncommitted-work`; only `merged` triggers worktree removal.
Factory code can supply role prompts and an `onLimit` callback for human guidance.

To replace durable behavior, bind a module outside the generated file:

```ts
import { bindDeliverySteps } from "@salimhamed/jigs/delivery";
import { deliverySteps } from "#jigs";
import { publishPullRequest } from "#steps/publish";

export const { deliverChange } = bindDeliverySteps({
  ...deliverySteps,
  openPullRequest: publishPullRequest,
});
```

`/agents`, `/linear`, and `/pull-requests` can also be bound independently.
A different ticket source can supply the delivery module's `WorkItem` directly.
See [the delivery guide](https://github.com/salimhamed/jigs/blob/main/docs/delivery.md)
for budgets, role overrides, and phase composition.

## Upgrading jigs

```sh
jigs upgrade              # or: jigs upgrade --to <version>
```

Upgrade installs the new package, refreshes `jigs.ts`, runs `jigs up`, and
checks custom factory code with `pnpm typecheck`. Review and commit the changes.
GitHub Packages requires a token in `~/.npmrc`; this factory's `.npmrc` only
routes the scope.

Changing a workflow or step's path or function name changes its durable id.
Finish or cancel affected active and parked runs before deploying those changes.
